1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,300
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:04,020
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:10,540
My name is Kathryn and today we're talking about a very, very important part of conversations in English.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:11,360
That's right.

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,640
Today we are introducing a new series all about small talk.

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,440
Okay, what is small talk, Marco?

7
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:30,120
Well, small talk is just everyday conversation about maybe the weather, about how you are, what's new in your life, etc. just plain conversation.

8
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:33,720
So something very simple and usually pretty quick.

9
00:00:33,980 --> 00:00:38,180
It's what you say to people when you bump into them or when you see them at a party.

10
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:43,740
So let's take a listen to today's dialogue and find out just what kind of small talk we have.

11
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:51,760
Hey Michelle, this is my friend James.

12
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:53,800
He's visiting Shanghai from New York.

13
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,360
Oh, hi James.

14
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:56,640
Nice to meet you.

15
00:00:57,140 --> 00:01:01,600
So are you visiting for business or pleasure?

16
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:03,700
Well, actually a little of both.

17
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:06,960
I'm meeting some business contacts but I'm also taking some Mandarin classes too.

18
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:07,860
Oh, that's cool.

19
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:08,780
How's it going?

20
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:11,760
Well, I'm finding the classes pretty tough actually.

21
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:13,680
But I'm having a great time in Shanghai.

22
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:15,300
It's really an amazing city.

23
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:16,340
It sure is.

24
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:17,740
Are you staying for long?

25
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:19,420
Only two weeks, unfortunately.

26
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:21,840
I wish I could stay longer but...

27
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:25,700
Well, listen, if you need someone to show you the sites, then just call me.

28
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,940
I'm having a little get-together at my new apartment next week.

29
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:30,900
So if you want to drop by then...

30
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:31,520
That sounds great.

31
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:32,220
I'd love to.

32
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:33,640
Let me take down your number, Michelle.

33
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:39,200
All right, we're back.

34
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:44,800
So now let's take a look at a lot of great phrases that we've picked out for you this time on Fluency Builder.

35
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:47,400
Fluency Builder.

36
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:48,960
All right.

37
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:50,680
So we've got two Fluency Builders today.

38
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:53,320
So we're going to be exploring a lot of different phrases.

39
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:54,780
We've got three now.

40
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,180
The first one is very, very common.

41
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,920
You hear this phrase, nice to meet you.

42
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:01,120
Right.

43
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:08,080
So obviously if you meet someone new, you're introduced by your friend or something, you can just say, hi, nice to meet you.

44
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:08,980
All right.

45
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:10,730
So, nice to meet you.

46
00:02:11,150 --> 00:02:12,170
Nice to meet you too.

47
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:16,710
That's what we say afterwards because it sounds very funny if you say it two times.

48
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,560
So, hi, it's nice to meet you.

49
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:19,890
Oh, it's nice to meet you too.

50
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:20,990
Right, exactly.

51
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:28,610
So she went on, this girl, Michelle, to ask if he is visiting Shanghai for business or pleasure.

52
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:29,480
All right.

53
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:33,820
This is an important difference, something that we often talk about in Small Talk.

54
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:35,720
You can travel for business.

55
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:37,280
That means business trips.

56
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:41,820
Or you can travel to see different sites and have fun.

57
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:42,420
That's called pleasure.

58
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:45,680
And so we often hear this phrase, business or pleasure.

59
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:46,680
Right.

60
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:50,400
So if someone comes to your country, you say, oh, so are you here for business or pleasure?

61
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:54,160
Oh, I'm here on business or I'm here to see the sites.

62
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:55,720
Okay, very good.

63
00:02:56,440 --> 00:03:00,930
And so, James is actually in Shanghai doing both things.

64
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:04,750
He's on a business trip, but he's also taking Mandarin classes.

65
00:03:05,210 --> 00:03:07,870
And so Michelle went on to say, that's great.

66
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:09,230
How's it going?

67
00:03:09,890 --> 00:03:10,290
All right.

68
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:12,270
You're going to hear this all the time in English.

69
00:03:12,270 --> 00:03:15,750
We say, how's it going to our friends and family?

70
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:23,680
I wouldn't say it in a formal occasion with my boss or with my teacher, but it's very casual, very informal.

71
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:25,420
And it's a way to ask how things are.

72
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:27,580
So you're taking classes.

73
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:29,760
How's it going?

74
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:30,100
Exactly.

75
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:34,000
So this basically means how are the classes going?

76
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:34,780
How is everything?

77
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:35,640
Right.

78
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,280
Or you could say, oh, Marco, I haven't seen you in a while.

79
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:39,280
How is it going?

80
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:43,180
And that's another way of saying hi to someone or saying, how are you?

81
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:44,000
How are you?

82
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:45,580
How have you been recently?

83
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:47,800
So there are two differences in that phrase there.

84
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:51,620
In this case, Michelle is asking how the Mandarin classes are going.

85
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:52,400
How is it?

86
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:53,400
Is it good?

87
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:53,960
Is it bad?

88
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:54,560
Is it difficult?

89
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:56,960
Is it easy?

90
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:57,960
Etc.

91
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,740
But if you say to someone, hi, I haven't seen you for such a long time.

92
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:01,440
How's it going?

93
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:03,700
That's more of a how are you type thing.

94
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:05,400
That's like how's your life going?

95
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:06,560
Exactly.

96
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:08,720
So just think about what you're talking about.

97
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:11,680
Are you talking about general situations like a person's life?

98
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:16,600
Or are you talking about maybe lessons or class or job?

99
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:17,320
That's right.

100
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:19,440
Okay, so we're going to take a break here.

101
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:23,300
Let's listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back in a little bit with more phrases.

102
00:04:29,670 --> 00:04:31,430
Hey Michelle, this is my friend James.

103
00:04:31,870 --> 00:04:33,510
He's visiting Shanghai from New York.

104
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:35,070
Oh, hi James.

105
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:36,350
Nice to meet you.

106
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:41,290
So are you visiting for business or pleasure?

107
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:43,410
Well, actually a little of both.

108
00:04:43,790 --> 00:04:46,670
I'm meeting some business contacts, but I'm also taking some Mandarin classes too.

109
00:04:46,970 --> 00:04:47,570
Oh, that's cool.

110
00:04:48,050 --> 00:04:48,490
How's it going?

111
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:53,410
Well, I'm finding the classes pretty tough actually, but I'm having a great time in Shanghai.

112
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:55,010
It's really an amazing city.

113
00:04:55,410 --> 00:04:56,050
It sure is.

114
00:04:56,610 --> 00:04:57,450
Are you staying for long?

115
00:04:57,890 --> 00:04:59,150
Only two weeks, unfortunately.

116
00:04:59,770 --> 00:05:01,570
I wish I could stay longer, but...

117
00:05:01,570 --> 00:05:05,410
Well, listen, if you need someone to show you the sites, then just call me.

118
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:08,650
I'm having a little get together at my new apartment next week.

119
00:05:08,890 --> 00:05:10,570
So if you want to drop by then...

120
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:11,230
That sounds great.

121
00:05:11,490 --> 00:05:11,930
I'd love to.

122
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:13,370
Let me take down your number, Michelle.

123
00:05:18,310 --> 00:05:18,770
All right, we're back.

124
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:22,080
So now let's take a look at Fluency Builder Part Two.

125
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:27,650
So Michelle, at the end of today's dialogue says, if you need someone to show you the sites, then just call me.

126
00:05:28,190 --> 00:05:28,390
All right.

127
00:05:28,390 --> 00:05:33,710
So to show someone the sites, we talked about this a little bit earlier with that word pleasure.

128
00:05:34,070 --> 00:05:36,050
What does it mean to show someone the sites, Marco?

129
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:42,130
So basically, the sites, we're talking about the different attractions of a city or place.

130
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:48,560
So the museums, maybe different buildings or different attractions in general.

131
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:50,280
What is there to sightsee?

132
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:55,640
Okay, so we are in Paris, Marco, and I say, hey, I'd love to show you the sites.

133
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:56,550
What do you expect to see?

134
00:05:56,810 --> 00:05:58,710
So maybe we would go to the Eiffel Tower.

135
00:05:59,570 --> 00:06:00,910
Maybe we would go to...

136
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:01,850
Notre Dame Cathedral.

137
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:02,490
Exactly.

138
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:10,400
And we would go see all these different sites and that would mean that you are showing me the sites.

139
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:13,000
You are taking me to see all these sites.

140
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,930
And she is also having a little get together at her apartment, at her new apartment.

141
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:19,850
So what is a little get together?

142
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:20,810
This is a noun.

143
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:22,600
This is a get together.

144
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:23,760
So think about this.

145
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:28,320
Two different words actually, get together, but it's a noun, it's a thing.

146
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:30,640
It's like a party, but it's a small party.

147
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:32,120
Right.

148
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:32,600
A little get together.

149
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:42,160
So I just, I like to think of a get together as something very casual, something very small, sometimes intimate, where you just have three or four or five friends.

150
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:46,360
And it's, yeah, it's not as big as a party where everyone's dancing.

151
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:46,670
Okay.

152
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:52,930
So a little get together is just a small group of people, you invite them over to your house, very relaxed, very casual.

153
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:54,310
It's not really a party.

154
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:57,200
It's more of a reunion of friends.

155
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:57,910
And like we said, it's a noun.

156
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,610
So you say, I'm having a get together, you should come.

157
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:03,040
Okay.

158
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:06,800
And she actually asks them to come, but she says it in a different way.

159
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,210
She says, if you want to drop by.

160
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:10,320
Okay.

161
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:18,820
This is something people often say in small talk, because you don't want to say, you should come stay for a while because that's, I don't know, they just met.

162
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:19,320
It's not in order.

163
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:19,880
Yeah.

164
00:07:20,020 --> 00:07:25,540
So you should drop by means you should come and see and decide if you want to stay.

165
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:34,530
So basically it's also kind of a relaxed way of saying you can come whenever you want during the, during our party or our get together.

166
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:40,560
So if the get together is 9pm to midnight, you can drop by whenever, 10 o'clock, 11 o'clock.

167
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:43,170
So to drop by is to come for a short period.

168
00:07:43,750 --> 00:07:52,170
So if we're talking on the phone and I say, I'll drop by your house later on today, that means I'm going to go to your house very quickly at an unexpected time.

169
00:07:52,450 --> 00:07:52,610
Yeah.

170
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,920
Or you could use it in a question, you know, Marco, I really wanted to borrow that DVD.

171
00:07:56,920 --> 00:07:58,450
When can I drop by to pick it up?

172
00:07:58,880 --> 00:07:59,090
Okay.

173
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:00,130
Very, very good.

174
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:03,240
So let's take a look at our last phrase.

175
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:09,470
Michelle and James decided that they want to keep in touch and so he said, let me take down your number.

176
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:10,500
Okay.

177
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:15,780
So we're not talking about taking it anywhere, taking a number anywhere and going somewhere.

178
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:17,680
We're talking about writing something down.

179
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:19,980
So in this case, your number is your phone number.

180
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:20,680
Okay.

181
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:24,880
Let me take down your number means let me write down your telephone number.

182
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:26,360
Uh huh.

183
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:27,360
Okay.

184
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:30,240
So it's another way of saying, let me write down your telephone number.

185
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:31,080
Take down my phone number.

186
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:31,600
Call me later.

187
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:33,320
That's right.

188
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:35,560
So can I take down your number so I can call you later?

189
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:36,100
Okay.

190
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:37,840
That's another way of asking this question.

191
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:38,500
Very good.

192
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:40,320
All right.

193
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:41,320
So that concludes Fluency Builder.

194
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:44,080
Let's listen to our dialogue one last time and we'll be back in a bit.

